    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:49b18e3fb3f9ec7b",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "2f454b03d4efa42fcfe98bfbe593cfd1ce729c4c776d4677b29040603ac5cb4e",
      "text": "Day 12 · Continue past the lagoons of Cañapa, Hedionda, Chiarkota and Honda, with a stop at the viewpoint of Ollagüe, an active volcano with smoke rising from its crater, then cross the Chiguana salt flat to the Salar de Uyuni, the largest salt flat on Earth: some 11,000 km² at 3,650 metres, holding around 10 billion tonnes of salt. In the rainy season, a thin layer of water turns it into the world’s largest mirror.\n\nSee how salt is extracted and processed in Colchani, visit the Ojos del Salar (“Eyes of Water”) and the Isla del Pescado with its giant cacti and views over the salt flat. Lunch box included. At the end of the tour, transfer to Uyuni.",
